;;; A relation parameter a derived activator can bind to `eq` heads a premise
;;; of one argument, and only a commitment derives it, so `ein saturate`
;;; answered where `ein solve` stopped — **W60**, register row
;;; `W60/eq-arity-under-commitment` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A rule `r` whose parameter `?R` heads `(?R ?x)`, and a rule `mk` that
;;; derives `r`'s activator from the marker `mark`, `(r ?f)`, wherever a `p`
;;; holds. `(mark eq)` passes `eq` on; no fact holds a `p`, so root derives
;;; no activator, and a commitment holding a candidate `(p …)` does.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `mk` can bind `?R` to `eq`, which `(mark eq)` writes where `mk` reads it,
;;; and `(?R ?x)` is then `(eq ?x)`, which holds no fact of equality: the
;;; program is refused at load (Q-M1f.46), whether or not a state derives the
;;; activator, as a derived activator that can bind a relation parameter to a
;;; fact is refused (Q-M1f.1), and every command that loads it exits 1 (`ein
;;; test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `ein saturate FILE --dump` answered root's fixpoint, exit 0; `ein solve`,
;;; `solve --minimal` and `ein oracle` stopped at the first commitment that
;;; derives `(r eq)`, where its plan is compiled, exit 1, with the compiler's
;;; *`(eq …)` takes exactly 2 arguments and was given 1* (at `ee3da657`,
;;; release and debug alike): two commands disagreed on one program.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The loader refuses the program: every command that loads it exits 1 with
;;; one line, naming `r`, `?R`, the form, `(mark eq)` and `mk` (`ein test`:
;;; 2), in either build. The register's `now` is that refusal; the section
;;; above is the record of the answer it replaced.
;;;

(relation p Node :why "{?1} is a p")
(relation hit Node :why "{?1} is hit")
(hit z)
(mark eq)
(rule r (?R)
  :match (?R ?x)
  :assert (hit ?x)
  :why "{?x} stands in {?R}, so it is hit")
(rule mk ()
  :match (and (p ?y) (mark ?f))
  :assert (r ?f)
  :why "{?y} is a p, so r is asked of {?f}")
